WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.500
For the last 10 years, pretty much everything I need to learn now is through Google, YouTube, and Wikipedia.

00:07.500 --> 00:16.800
I've never needed to go to a lecture, a presentation, and I feel a little bit odd being in this situation now,

00:16.800 --> 00:23.500
given how I think learning happens these days, at least from my point of view.

00:23.500 --> 00:30.800
And most of everything I learn, when I reflect on it, happened in chunks of 4 minutes and 12 seconds.

00:30.800 --> 00:37.000
4 minutes and 12 seconds is the average video length of a YouTube video.

00:37.000 --> 00:44.800
And I do notice that music videos seem to operate around that mark, and my attention span does seem to be held in that genre, music video.

00:44.800 --> 00:55.800
When I listen to lectures, and in fact I've got a link to a lecture called 'An Anthropological Introduction to YouTube' by Michael Wesch from Kansas,

00:55.800 --> 01:04.200
it's a 60-minute long video to the Library of Congress. He won the Professor of the Year in 2009,

01:04.200 --> 01:11.800
and it does give us an anthropological view of the phenomenon of YouTube. But it's 60 minutes long.

01:11.800 --> 01:21.000
But when I listened to it again last night to check if I would share it with you—the link to it that is—

01:21.000 --> 01:29.000
I noticed again, all the points he's making come in 4-minute chunks, round about.

01:29.000 --> 01:37.500
So in fact, it's a video 60 minutes long made up of however many 4-minute 12-second videos can fit in that time span.

01:37.500 --> 01:49.000
On the other hand, when I listen to an Echo360 of your average educationally untrained, but professionally trained, lecture,

01:49.000 --> 01:56.800
that chunking of points is not so clear. Often one point can ramble on for 15 minutes, or more.

01:56.800 --> 02:00.000
So I'm going on the premise that all learning happens today, at least, in 4 minutes and 12 seconds.
